---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const pageTitle = "Uniswap v3 Impermanent Loss Formula & Fee APR Math";
const pageDescription = "Rigorous quantitative breakdown of Uniswap v3 concentrated liquidity impermanent loss formulas, virtual reserve geometry, and empirical breakeven tables comparing 0.05% vs 0.30% fee tiers.";
const canonical = "https://site-19-nine.vercel.app/impermanent-loss-vs-fee-apr-uniswap-v3-formula/";

const schema = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "TechArticle",
      "headline": "Uniswap v3 Impermanent Loss Formula & Exact Fee APR Math Breakdown",
      "description": pageDescription,
      "url": canonical,
      "datePublished": "2026-09-09",
      "dateModified": "2026-09-14",
      "author": {
        "@type": "Organization",
        "name": "GreekVisualizer Quantitative Research",
        "url": "https://site-19-nine.vercel.app/"
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      "itemListElement": [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 1,
          "name": "Home",
          "item": "https://site-19-nine.vercel.app/"
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 2,
          "name": "AMM Mathematics",
          "item": canonical
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How does Uniswap v3 modify the classical impermanent loss formula?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In Uniswap v2, impermanent loss is strictly governed by IL = 2*sqrt(k)/(1+k) - 1 where k = P_1/P_0. In Uniswap v3, virtual reserve boundaries [p_a, p_b] concentrate real capital, effectively multiplying the standard v2 divergence loss by the capital leverage factor 1 / (1 - sqrt(p_a / p_b)) within the active price tick."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What daily volume does a 0.05% Uniswap v3 pool need to overcome a 20% price divergence?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a typical +/-15% concentrated position with 2.35% impermanent loss and $20M pool TVL, a 0.05% pool requires approximately $20M in daily trading volume to break even in 47 days, whereas a 0.30% pool at the same volume achieves breakeven in just 7.8 days."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why is concentrated liquidity mathematically equivalent to an exotic short straddle or short put?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Providing concentrated liquidity yields constant positive theta (fee income) while bearing negative gamma (divergence loss as price moves away from center). When price moves outside the tick bounds [p_a, p_b], the position converts entirely to the depreciated asset, mimicking an assigned cash-secured short put."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "At what turnover ratio does the 0.30% tier outperform the 0.05% tier for ETH/USDC pairs?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "When pool trading turnover (Volume / TVL) exceeds 0.25 on a 0.30% tier, the annualized fee income overcomes market divergence drag significantly faster than a 0.05% pool with turnover under 1.5, amortizing 15% price divergence in under 12 days."
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
});
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} canonical={canonical} schemaJson={schema}>
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto pt-6 pb-20">
    <!-- Breadcrumbs -->
    <nav class="flex items-center space-x-2 text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 mb-6">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-emerald-400 transition">Visualizer Hub</a>
      <span>/</span>
      <span class="text-slate-200">AMM Quant Math</span>
      <span>/</span>
      <span class="text-emerald-400">Uniswap v3 Impermanent Loss</span>
    </nav>

    <!-- Exactly ONE H1 Tag -->
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-black text-white tracking-tight leading-tight mb-6">
      Uniswap v3 Impermanent Loss Formula &amp; Exact Fee APR Math Breakdown
    </h1>

    <div class="flex items-center space-x-4 text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 border-b border-slate-800/80 pb-6 mb-8">
      <span>Published: September 2026</span>
      <span>•</span>
      <span>Reading Time: 18 min</span>
      <span>•</span>
      <span class="text-emerald-400">Concentrated AMM Derivatives Math</span>
    </div>

    <!-- 45-60 Word Featured Snippet Quick Answer Box with Green Accent Border -->
    <div class="border-l-4 border-emerald-500 bg-[#0B1324]/90 rounded-r-xl p-5 sm:p-6 text-left shadow-xl shadow-emerald-500/5 my-8">
      <div class="flex items-center space-x-2 text-emerald-400 font-mono text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
        <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-emerald-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/>
        </svg>
        <h2 class="text-xs font-mono font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-400 inline">
          Quick Answer &bull; Concentrated AMM Impermanent Loss Mechanics
        </h2>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-200 leading-relaxed font-sans">
        Uniswap v3 amplifies impermanent loss by concentrating liquidity within finite price boundaries, multiplying classical constant-product divergence loss by the leverage factor 1/(1 - sqrt(pa/pb)). Accumulated fee yield overcomes this divergence risk only when pool trading volume generates an annualized fee APR exceeding the position's structural negative gamma decay rate.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 text-xs font-mono text-cyan-400">
        <span>&bull; Formula: IL_v3 = [V_LP(P) - V_hold(P)] / V_hold(P)</span>
        <span>&bull; Gamma Profile: Convex Short Volatility</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Content Body -->
    <div class="space-y-12 text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base mt-10">

      <!-- Section 1: Mathematical Derivation -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-emerald-400"></span>
          <span>1. Mathematical Derivation: From Infinite Invariant to Bounded Virtual Reserves</span>
        </h2>
        <p>
          In a classical Uniswap v2 automated market maker, liquidity is distributed continuously across infinite price space <code class="font-mono text-emerald-400">x &middot; y = k</code>. Let <code class="font-mono text-slate-200">P_0</code> denote the entry price of token X denominated in token Y, and let price evolve by ratio <code class="font-mono text-slate-200">k = P_1 / P_0</code>. The classical impermanent loss equation is:
        </p>

        <div class="bg-slate-900/90 border border-slate-800 p-5 rounded-xl font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-cyan-300 my-4">
          IL_v2(k) = (V_LP(k) - V_hold(k)) / V_hold(k) = (2 &middot; &radic;k) / (1 + k) - 1
        </div>

        <p>
          Uniswap v3 establishes capital concentration by confining token reserves within a discrete interval bounded by lower tick price <code class="font-mono text-emerald-400">p_a</code> and upper tick price <code class="font-mono text-emerald-400">p_b</code> where <code class="font-mono text-emerald-400">0 &lt; p_a &lt; p_b &lt; &infin;</code>. The invariant curve is translated along both axes by <em>virtual reserves</em>:
        </p>

        <div class="bg-slate-900/90 border border-slate-800 p-5 rounded-xl font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-emerald-400 my-4">
          (x + x_v) &middot; (y + y_v) = L&sup2;
          <br/><br/>
          where &nbsp; x_v = L / &radic;p_b &nbsp; and &nbsp; y_v = L &middot; &radic;p_a
        </div>

        <p>
          Here, <code class="font-mono text-slate-200">L</code> represents the active virtual liquidity depth parameter: <code class="font-mono text-cyan-300">L = &radic;(k_real)</code>. For any instantaneous spot price <code class="font-mono text-slate-200">P = y / x</code> within active bounds <code class="font-mono text-emerald-400">p_a &le; P &le; p_b</code>, the real token inventories held in the pool position are derived analytically:
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-4 font-mono text-xs">
          <div class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800">
            <span class="text-cyan-400 block font-bold mb-1">Real Token X Inventory:</span>
            x(P) = L &middot; (1 / &radic;P - 1 / &radic;p_b)
          </div>
          <div class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800">
            <span class="text-emerald-400 block font-bold mb-1">Real Token Y Inventory:</span>
            y(P) = L &middot; (&radic;P - &radic;p_a)
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          When spot price crosses the lower threshold (<code class="font-mono text-rose-400">P &le; p_a</code>), all token Y is fully depleted and the position converts 100% into token X: <code class="font-mono text-xs text-slate-300">x_max = L &middot; (1/&radic;p_a - 1/&radic;p_b)</code>. Conversely, when spot price breaches the upper threshold (<code class="font-mono text-emerald-400">P &ge; p_b</code>), all token X is converted into token Y: <code class="font-mono text-xs text-slate-300">y_max = L &middot; (&radic;p_b - &radic;p_a)</code>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: Portfolio Value Function & Closed Form IL -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-emerald-400"></span>
          <span>2. Portfolio Value Function &amp; Concentrated Impermanent Loss Closed-Form Equation</span>
        </h2>
        <p>
          To establish the closed-form impermanent loss equation, we evaluate the total market value of the concentrated position in terms of numeraire token Y: <code class="font-mono text-cyan-300">V_v3(P) = x(P) &middot; P + y(P)</code> across all three piecewise price regimes:
        </p>

        <div class="bg-slate-950 border border-slate-800 p-5 rounded-xl font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-200 my-4">
          <strong class="text-emerald-400 block mb-2">Piecewise Portfolio Value V_v3(P):</strong>
          &bull; Regime 1 (P &lt; p_a): &nbsp; V_v3(P) = L &middot; P &middot; (1/&radic;p_a - 1/&radic;p_b)<br/>
          &bull; Regime 2 (p_a &le; P &le; p_b): V_v3(P) = L &middot; [2 &middot; &radic;P - P / &radic;p_b - &radic;p_a]<br/>
          &bull; Regime 3 (P &gt; p_b): &nbsp; V_v3(P) = L &middot; (&radic;p_b - &radic;p_a)
        </div>

        <p>
          Next, consider the benchmark "Hold" strategy where the liquidity provider merely holds the initial inventory deposited at entry price <code class="font-mono text-slate-200">P_0</code>:
        </p>

        <div class="bg-slate-900/90 border border-slate-800 p-5 rounded-xl font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-cyan-300 my-4">
          V_hold(P) = x(P_0) &middot; P + y(P_0) = L &middot; [ P &middot; (1/&radic;P_0 - 1/&radic;p_b) + (&radic;P_0 - &radic;p_a) ]
        </div>

        <p>
          The exact impermanent loss ratio is defined by relative underperformance: <code class="font-mono text-emerald-400">IL_v3(P) = [V_v3(P) - V_hold(P)] / V_hold(P)</code>. Substituting the equations inside active range <code class="font-mono text-emerald-400">p_a &le; P &le; p_b</code> yields:
        </p>

        <div class="bg-slate-950 border border-emerald-500/40 p-5 rounded-xl font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-emerald-300 my-4">
          IL_v3(P) = - L &middot; &radic;P_0 &middot; (&radic;k - 1)&sup2; / [ P &middot; (1/&radic;P_0 - 1/&radic;p_b) + (&radic;P_0 - &radic;p_a) ]
        </div>

        <p>
          Notice the algebraic relationship to Uniswap v2: the numerator matches the absolute dollar divergence loss of Uniswap v2, but the denominator is reduced by the capital efficiency factor <code class="font-mono text-amber-400">&eta;</code>:
        </p>

        <div class="bg-slate-900/90 border border-slate-800 p-4 rounded-lg font-mono text-xs text-amber-300">
          &eta; = 1 / [ 1 - &radic;(p_a / p_b) ]
        </div>

        <p>
          Consequently, a tight tick range with <code class="font-mono text-slate-200">p_a / p_b = 0.81</code> yields <code class="font-mono text-amber-400">&eta; = 1 / (1 - 0.90) = 10&times;</code>. <strong>The LP achieves 10x higher fee APR on active capital, but simultaneously suffers 10x magnified percentage impermanent loss</strong> relative to deposited principal.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: Options Greeks Correspondence -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-emerald-400"></span>
          <span>3. Options Greeks Correspondence: Negative Gamma, Positive Theta &amp; Volatility Drag</span>
        </h2>
        <p>
          In quantitative derivatives finance, a Uniswap v3 concentrated liquidity position is mathematically isomorphic to a covered short strangle or short put option strategy. Taking the second derivative of portfolio value <code class="font-mono text-cyan-300">V_v3(P)</code> with respect to asset price <code class="font-mono text-slate-200">P</code> reveals the position's structural gamma:
        </p>

        <div class="bg-slate-950 border border-slate-800 p-5 rounded-xl font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-rose-400 my-4">
          &Gamma;_v3(P) = &part;&sup2;V_v3 / &part;P&sup2; = - L / (2 &middot; P^(3/2)) &lt; 0 &nbsp; &forall; P &isin; [p_a, p_b]
        </div>

        <p>
          Because gamma is strictly negative everywhere within the active interval, the liquidity provider is intrinsically <strong>short realized volatility</strong>. The position suffers curvature drag whenever the price moves in either direction away from entry. To compensate the LP for this negative gamma profile, the pool pays continuous cash flows via swap fees&mdash;directly analogous to positive option theta (<code class="font-mono text-emerald-400">&Theta; &gt; 0</code>).
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-4 font-mono text-xs">
          <div class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800">
            <span class="text-cyan-400 block font-bold mb-1">Delta Profile (&Delta;):</span>
            &part;V / &part;P = L &middot; (1/&radic;P - 1/&radic;p_b)<br/>
            Decreases monotonically from 1.0 down to 0.0 as price rises.
          </div>
          <div class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800">
            <span class="text-rose-400 block font-bold mb-1">Gamma Profile (&Gamma;):</span>
            &Gamma; = - L / (2 &middot; P^(3/2))<br/>
            Maximum negative curvature concentrated at lowest spot prices.
          </div>
          <div class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800">
            <span class="text-emerald-400 block font-bold mb-1">Theta Yield (&Theta;):</span>
            dFees / dt = (Volume &times; FeeTier) &times; (L_pos / L_pool)<br/>
            Continuous positive cash flow offsetting curvature drag.
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4: Fee APR Mechanics -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-emerald-400"></span>
          <span>4. Fee APR Mechanics &amp; Liquidity Density: Capital Efficiency vs Out-of-Range Risk</span>
        </h2>
        <p>
          A liquidity provider earns fees exclusively while price resides inside active tick bounds. The annualized fee APR is computed by integrating swap volume across the active tick interval:
        </p>

        <div class="bg-slate-900/90 border border-slate-800 p-5 rounded-xl font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-cyan-300 my-4">
          Fee APR = [ Daily Volume &times; Fee Tier &times; (L_pos / L_pool_active) &times; 365 ] / Capital_Invested
        </div>

        <p>
          For an investment of $100,000 deployed into a pool with $20,000,000 in active tick TVL, our position controls <code class="font-mono text-emerald-400">0.50%</code> of pool liquidity. The fundamental strategic trade-off is choosing between the <strong>0.05% fee tier</strong> (5 bps, standard for stable or highly correlated pairs with high turnover) and the <strong>0.30% fee tier</strong> (30 bps, standard for volatile pairs with lower turnover).
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5: Empirical Shootout Table -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-emerald-400"></span>
          <span>5. Empirical Crossover Tables: 0.05% vs 0.30% Fee Tiers Across Volatility Regimes</span>
        </h2>
        <p>
          To demonstrate the exact crossover point where fee income overcomes impermanent loss, we model a standard concentrated ETH/USDC range of <code class="font-mono text-slate-200">[0.80 &middot; P_0, 1.25 &middot; P_0]</code> on a $100,000 capital base across multiple price divergence levels (5%, 10%, 15%, 20%, 30%, and 50%) and pool daily volume tiers:
        </p>

        <!-- Extensive Multi-Row Crossover Table -->
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-[#0F172A] my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-xs sm:text-sm text-left border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-800 bg-slate-950/80 text-slate-400 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase">
                <th class="py-3.5 px-3">Price Move (k)</th>
                <th class="py-3.5 px-3 text-rose-400">IL Loss ($)</th>
                <th class="py-3.5 px-3">Daily Volume</th>
                <th class="py-3.5 px-3 text-cyan-400">0.05% Daily Fee (APR)</th>
                <th class="py-3.5 px-3 text-cyan-400">0.05% Breakeven</th>
                <th class="py-3.5 px-3 text-emerald-400">0.30% Daily Fee (APR)</th>
                <th class="py-3.5 px-3 text-emerald-400">0.30% Breakeven</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800/60 text-slate-300 font-mono text-xs">
              <tr>
